## 8️⃣ Real-Life Example: Calculator Object with Methods

```javascript
let calculator = {
  add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
  },
  subtract(a, b) {
    return a - b;
  },
  multiply(a, b) {
    return a * b;
  },
  divide(a, b) {
    if (b === 0) {
      return "Error! Division by zero.";
    }
    return a / b;
  }
};

console.log(calculator.add(5, 3));      // 8
console.log(calculator.subtract(5, 3)); // 2
console.log(calculator.multiply(5, 3)); // 15
console.log(calculator.divide(5, 0));   // Error! Division by zero.
```


## 5️⃣ Different Ways to Write Methods

### Traditional syntax:

```javascript
let obj = {
  methodName: function () {
    // code here
  }
};
```

### ES6 shorthand syntax:

```javascript
let obj = {
  methodName() {
    // code here
  }
};
```

Both are valid and do the same thing.

